        updatedAt: '2025',
        title: 'How to File Schedule C (Self-Employment Tax Form)',
        metaTitle: 'How to File Schedule C: Step-by-Step Guide for Freelancers',
        description: 'Schedule C guide for freelancers and side hustlers: reporting income, claiming business expenses, the home office deduction, self-employment tax, and record-keeping that survives an audit.',
        category: 'taxes',
        audience: ['Native', 'Immigrant'],
        difficulty: 'Medium',
        totalTime: '2–4 hours',
        totalCost: 'Free (self-file) to $150+ (preparer)',
        summary: 'Schedule C is where sole proprietors and single-member LLCs report business income and expenses on their Form 1040. Good records make it a 2-hour job; the deductions are worth more than most people expect.',
        documents: [
            'All 1099-NEC and 1099-K forms from clients and platforms',
            'Records of income without a 1099 (cash, direct payments)',
            'Receipts and statements for every business expense',
            'Mileage log or vehicle expenses (if you drive for work)',
            'Home office measurements (square footage) and utility costs',
        ],
        steps: [
            { phase: 'before', title: 'Confirm Schedule C is right for you', detail: 'Use Schedule C if you are a sole proprietor, freelancer, gig worker, or single-member LLC. Partners and multi-member LLCs use Schedule K-1 (Form 1065) instead; S-corp owners take wages plus a K-1.', duration: '15 min' },
            { phase: 'before', title: 'Total your business income', detail: 'Add up all 1099-NECs, 1099-Ks (payment platforms), and any income paid without a form. The IRS matches reported 1099s to your return automatically — unreported income generates a CP2000 notice with penalties.', duration: '30–60 min' },
            { phase: 'fill', title: 'Categorize expenses (Part II)', detail: 'Common categories: advertising, car expenses, supplies, contract labor, insurance, legal/professional services, rent, software subscriptions, phone/internet (business share), travel, meals (50% limit). Keep personal costs out — commingling is the fastest route to an audit.', duration: '1–2 hours' },
            { phase: 'fill', title: 'Claim the home office deduction if eligible', detail: 'The simplified method: $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft ($1,500 max), no receipts needed. The regular method: actual expenses (rent, utilities, insurance) times the business-use percentage. Either way the space must be used regularly and exclusively for business.', duration: '30 min' },
            { phase: 'fill', title: 'Calculate net profit and self-employment tax', detail: 'Income minus expenses = net profit (Line 31). If it is $400+, Schedule SE calculates self-employment tax: 15.3% covering Social Security and Medicare — the part employers normally pay for employees. You deduct half of it on your 1040.', duration: '20 min' },
            { phase: 'submit', title: 'Attach to Form 1040 and file', detail: 'Schedule C, Schedule SE, and Form 1040 file together. Most tax software handles this automatically in its self-employment tier. E-file and choose direct deposit for the fastest refund.', duration: '30 min' },
            { phase: 'followup', title: 'Pay quarterly estimates next year', detail: 'If you expect to owe $1,000+, make quarterly estimated payments (April, June, September, January) using Form 1040-ES to avoid underpayment penalties.', duration: '30 min/quarter' },
        ],
        faqs: [
            { q: 'Can I deduct my phone and internet on Schedule C?', a: 'Yes, but only the business-use percentage. If you use your phone 60% for work, deduct 60% of the bill. Keep a usage log for at least a month to back up your estimate.' },
            { q: 'What happens if I report a loss?', a: 'Losses are legitimate and often deductible against other income — but repeated losses year after year make the IRS question whether it is a business or a hobby. The hobby-loss rule presumes profit in 3 of 5 years; keep records showing a genuine profit motive.' },
            { q: 'Do DoorDash and Uber drivers file Schedule C?', a: 'Yes. Platform work is self-employment income. The platform issues a 1099-NEC and/or 1099-K, and you report it on Schedule C — where you can deduct mileage (67¢/mile for 2024), which usually saves more than the standard vehicle expense method.' },
        ],
